# --- mtheme v2 (#711) ---------------------------------------------------
# Elevation model: four levels, each DEFINED as a (surface, border) pair.
# 0 sunken / 1 base / 2 raised / 3 overlay. In this dark scheme, higher is a
# lighter tint; level 3 additionally takes color.border_strong. There are no
# shadows; see decor.* below.
#
# --- Loop 1 colour-scheme pass (designer 4/6): what changed here --------
# Two real defects fixed, plus three legacy keys re-aligned to the semantic
# tokens they were meant to track. Full numbers and the legacy-key mapping
# table are in the Loop 1 report; short version:
#  1. color.focus_ring was literally color.accent (0x2563C9). Measured
#     against color.surface_raised it was 2.72:1, under this family's own
#     3:1 floor. A distinct, brighter tint of the SAME accent hue (~217 deg
#     in both cases) fixes it without inventing a second accent colour:
#     0x4C8EFF measures 5.46:1 on surface and 4.86:1 on surface_raised.
#  2. color.danger (0xFF453A) put on_danger/danger at 3.41:1, short of the
#     4.5:1 AA floor this file's other body-text pairs all clear. Deepened
#     to 0x00D93A2F (hue unchanged, ~4 deg, still unmistakably red, not the
#     accent blue) for 4.57:1.
#  3. close_button/close_button_hover/color_error re-pointed at the new
#     danger red so the legacy keys and color.danger agree again.
#  4. desktop_bg (0x16161A) and taskbar_bg (0x1F1F25) were near-misses of
#     color.surface_sunken and color.titlebar_bottom rather than exact
#     matches, the only two places this file's legacy block wasn't
#     bit-identical with its semantic equivalent. Set equal for coherency;
#     the visual delta is under 2% luminance, imperceptible on its own.
#
# --- Loop 2 colour-scheme pass (designer 4/6): what changed here --------
# Director redirect B1/B2/B3/B10 plus C6. Full numbers, both luminance
# metrics, and the legacy-key mapping table are in the Loop 2 report; short
# version:
#  1. B1/B2 (color.accent, color.on_accent, color.sel_bg, color.sel_fg):
#     already correct from Loop 1, unchanged this loop. accent=0x2563C9,
#     on_accent=0xFFFFFF (5.67:1), sel_bg=accent, sel_fg=on_accent (same
#     5.67:1). Verified, not re-derived.
#  2. B3 (secondary label text): color.on_surface_muted moved from
#     0x9BA1AA to the director-mandated 0x9AA3AF (6.79:1 on color.surface,
#     was 6.66:1; both pass, this is the settled shared value, not a new
#     defect fix). Every literal copy in this file updated in lockstep:
#     state.field_placeholder_fg, state.scrollbar_thumb_hover,
#     btn.segmented.rest.fg.
#  3. Gradient delta fix on color.titlebar_top: the surfaces family (Loop 1
#     comment further down this file) flagged the active titlebar gradient
#     using an HSL-lightness metric (25.5% swing, over the ~12% budget).
#     Re-measured here with WCAG relative luminance (Y), the metric used
#     for every contrast ratio elsewhere in this file: the OLD pair
#     (0x30303A -> 0x24242B) was actually 40.6% by that metric, worse than
#     flagged, not better. The Loop 1 CHANGELOG entry's claimed "1.2%" for
#     this same pair was a miscalculation; corrected here, not repeated.
#     New color.titlebar_top = 0x26262F keeps titlebar_bottom (0x24242B)
#     untouched, same hue family (blue-gray, +9 offset on B, matching the
#     rest of the palette's +7..+10 pattern), lands at 9.6% relative-
#     luminance swing (and 7.1% by the HSL-lightness metric too, so it
#     clears BOTH definitions with margin, not just whichever one is
#     convenient). Direction unchanged: top still lighter than bottom.
#  4. B10 (terminal ANSI palette per-scheme): NOT implemented in this file.
#     kernel/gui/terminal.c and userland/apps/terminal/main.c both hold a
#     static const ansi_colors[16] array; there is no ansi.* key, no
#     reader, no consumer anywhere in the theme engine. Adding ansi.* keys
#     here would be exactly the dead-data mistake Section 11 exists to
#     stop (0 grep hits for a reader = 0 keys added). This needs an engine
#     hook (an ansi.0..15 reader in kernel/gui/themes.c plus both terminal
#     call sites switched from the literal array to it) before a colour
#     file can carry it; flagged to the director/substrate owner, see
#     report. The two literal values the director specified for when that
#     hook exists: light-scheme green 0x007A00 (5.6:1 on white), cyan
#     0x007878 (5.3:1 on white); dark scheme values were already fine per
#     the director and need no change.
#  5. C6 (selected-swatch indicator, 2px on_accent inner ring + 1px outer
#     contrast line): the draw call WAS kernel/gui/settings/panel_appearance.c
#     draw_color_swatch(), which hardcoded a single 1px 0xFFFFFF ring. #703
#     deleted kernel/gui/settings/ (the unified panel framework had zero
#     callers; the shipping Settings is the userland /APPS/SETTINGS), so this
#     item now has no kernel draw call to change. This
#     family writes color hex values only, never C source (own brief,
#     restated at the top of the Loop 1 header above); the fix is a ~6-line
#     C edit, not a token file edit. Both required colours already exist as
#     tokens: color.on_accent for the 2px inner ring (unused in that file
#     today). For the 1px outer line I measured three candidates against
#     color.surface_raised (the swatch grid's panel background) before
#     picking one, because a first pass here used border_strong without
#     measuring it and that number (1.63:1) turned out to be too low to
#     trust for an implementer to build against:
#       color.border_strong        1.63:1 (too close to the panel, rejected)
#       color.on_surface_disabled  2.54:1 (still short of a 3:1 floor)
#       color.on_surface_muted     6.04:1 (comfortably clears 3:1, and it
#                                   is a mid-tone, not near-white, so it
#                                   still frames the ring against a light or
#                                   near-white swatch fill too, not only
#                                   against the dark panel)
#     Recommended: color.on_surface_muted for the outer line. Spec handed
#     to the Settings/panel implementer in the Loop 2 report: replace the
#     single 0xFFFFFF ring with SYS_THEME_COLOR(on_accent) at the swatch
#     bounds (2px) then SYS_THEME_COLOR(on_surface_muted) 1px further out,
#     same nested-fill ordering Family 2/3 already use for buttons/inputs.
color.surface_sunken=0x00121216
color.surface=0x001A1A1F
color.surface_raised=0x0024242B
color.surface_overlay=0x002C2C35
color.on_surface=0x00F2F3F5
color.on_surface_muted=0x009AA3AF
color.on_surface_disabled=0x005D636C
color.accent=0x002563C9
color.on_accent=0x00FFFFFF
color.accent_hover=0x003170D8
color.accent_active=0x001B4FA6
color.danger=0x00D93A2F
color.on_danger=0x00FFFFFF
color.border_subtle=0x002E2E37
color.border_strong=0x00454551
color.focus_ring=0x004C8EFF
color.sel_bg=0x002563C9
color.sel_fg=0x00FFFFFF
color.titlebar_top=0x0026262F
color.titlebar_bottom=0x0024242B
color.titlebar_inactive_top=0x001F1F25
color.titlebar_inactive_bottom=0x001A1A1F
color.titlebar_text=0x00F2F3F5
color.titlebar_text_inactive=0x008A9099
# titlebar_text_inactive WIRED #711 loop 2 (was parsed since loop 1, zero
# consumers): window.c now swaps to this ink for an unfocused window's title,
# gated to decor.style != beveled (retro_unix's own titlebar_text_inactive=
# 0xFFFFFF, set identical to its active value, would otherwise visibly change
# retro_unix - out of scope, B8/director spec covers modern only, active
# titlebar ink is untouched in both cases per that same spec).

# state.* : rest / hover / active / focus / disabled / selected, all explicit.
# None of these may be expressed as "the same colour at 20% alpha": there is
# no per-pixel alpha in this renderer to express it with.
state.btn_rest_bg=0x0024242B
state.btn_rest_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.btn_hover_bg=0x002E2E37
state.btn_hover_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.btn_active_bg=0x00191920
state.btn_active_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.btn_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF
state.btn_disabled_bg=0x0024242B
state.btn_disabled_fg=0x005D636C
state.btn_selected_bg=0x002563C9
state.btn_selected_fg=0x00FFFFFF
# --- #711 Loop 2 / Family 2 (Buttons): the four border keys the
# generic matrix never carried, plus the B9 accent-fill focus-ring fix.
# Additive only, same key family, wired the moment the parser table
# g_theme_fields[] and theme_t (kernel/gui/themes.c / themes.h) grow six
# entries mirroring the state.btn_* pattern already there - no renderer
# change needed beyond that, see report.
state.btn_rest_border=0x00454551      # = color.border_strong
state.btn_hover_border=0x00454551     # = color.border_strong (unchanged; fill lifts)
state.btn_active_border=0x00454551    # = color.border_strong
state.btn_disabled_border=0x002E2E37  # = color.border_subtle
state.btn_selected_border=0x001B4FA6  # = color.accent_active
# B9 (Loop 2, settled): a focus ring drawn in color.focus_ring on a
# button whose OWN fill is color.accent reads as no ring at all - in
# this theme color.focus_ring literally equals color.accent bit-for-bit
# (see color.* above), so state.btn_focus_ring on the accent-filled
# selected state would be a same-colour ring on a same-colour button,
# completely invisible. state.btn_focus_ring (already wired) stays the
# ring for rest/hover/active/disabled, none of which are accent-filled.
state.btn_selected_focus_ring=0x00FFFFFF  # = color.on_accent, per B9
state.item_rest_bg=0x002C2C35
state.item_rest_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.item_hover_bg=0x00343442
state.item_hover_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.item_active_bg=0x002563C9
state.item_active_fg=0x00FFFFFF
state.item_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF
state.item_disabled_bg=0x002C2C35
state.item_disabled_fg=0x005D636C
state.item_selected_bg=0x002563C9
state.item_selected_fg=0x00FFFFFF
state.input_rest_bg=0x00121216
state.input_rest_fg=0x00F2F3F5
state.input_rest_border=0x009AA3AF   # #711 loop2 (Family 3): was 0x454551 (= color.border_strong,
                                   # 1.98:1 against state.input_rest_bg/color.surface_sunken -
                                   # under the 3:1 non-text UI floor). Repointed at
                                   # color.on_surface_muted instead (7.32:1 on surface_sunken, resynced to the B3-updated on_surface_muted),
                                   # still an existing token, no invented hex. This is the box
                                   # border for text field / checkbox / dropdown-closed at rest
                                   # AND (via the literal copy below) the radio ring at rest -
                                   # one fix, four controls.
state.input_hover_border=0x005D636C
state.input_active_border=0x002563C9
state.input_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF
state.input_disabled_bg=0x00121216
state.input_disabled_fg=0x005D636C
state.input_selected_bg=0x002563C9
state.input_selected_fg=0x00FFFFFF

# metric.* : geometry, in pixels. Modern defaults per the #711 scales.
# NOT this designer's family (colour schemes only) - values untouched.
metric.titlebar_h=28
metric.border_w=1
metric.btn_h=28
metric.input_h=28
metric.pad=12
metric.gap=8
metric.focus_w=2
metric.focus_offset=1                     # #711 Loop 2 / surfaces (B9): ring
                                           #   sits 1px OUTSIDE the control's
                                           #   own bounds. New key, unwired.
                                           #   Re-applied after a concurrent
                                           #   write reverted it (see blame.md).
metric.winmenu_roww=224
metric.winmenu_rowh=28
metric.winmenu_hdr=24
metric.titlebar_btn=20
metric.titlebar_btn_gap=4
metric.grip=10
metric.scrollbar_w=16
metric.menu_row_h=28

# radius.* : none=0 sm=3 md=6 lg=10 . Window OUTER corners are always 0: the
# frame is a kernel-drawn rect over wallpaper and there is no alpha compositing
# to round it against.
# #711 Loop 2 / surfaces (director redirect B6, settled): radius.btn/input/
# menu move 6 -> 4px. These are WIRED keys (kernel/gui/themes.c
# g_theme_fields: TM_RADIUS_BTN/INPUT/MENU) so this is a LIVE value change.
# RE-APPLIED after a concurrent write on this shared file reverted it back
# to 6 the first time this loop (see blame.md, "a second write can silently
# revert a first" entry) - checked with grep immediately before re-editing.
radius.btn=4
radius.input=4
radius.menu=4
radius.card=10

# decor.* : beveled | flat | gradient. The only gradient this renderer has is
# a 2-stop vertical linear one, and the two stops are color.titlebar_top /
# color.titlebar_bottom above. There are no shadows in this system: window
# shadows were removed by explicit decision (#189) and elevation is expressed
# as a (surface token, border token) pair, never as a shadow.
# NOT this designer's family - values untouched.
decor.style=gradient
decor.titlebar_gradient=1
decor.grip=1
# decor.grip WIRED #711 loop 2 (parsed since #711 substrate, zero consumers
# before this loop): window.c's resize-grip draw block now also checks
# TM_DECOR_GRIP, not just WINDOW_FLAG_RESIZABLE. 1 reproduces the prior
# always-on behaviour for every theme that does not set the key.

# type.* : whole px on the glyph-cache ladder; lineheight = round(px*1.4);
# regular and faux-bold only. No body text below 12px, no caption below 11px:
# text is whole-pixel positioned and unhinted. NOT this designer's family.
type.caption=11
type.caption_lineheight=15
type.caption_weight=regular
type.body=14
type.body_lineheight=20
type.body_weight=regular
type.title=16
type.title_lineheight=22
type.title_weight=regular
type.heading=20
type.heading_lineheight=28
type.heading_weight=bold
type.display=28
type.display_lineheight=39
type.display_weight=bold

# --- window-decoration live-reload fix (#711 loop 2, designer 1) ---------
# window.c cached win->border_color and win->titlebar_color once at window
# creation (border_color: never refreshed again; titlebar_color: refreshed
# only on focus change), so an ALREADY-OPEN window's frame border, and a
# beveled-theme window's flat titlebar fill, went stale on a live theme
# switch until the next focus change or window recreate. No app in the tree
# writes either field per-window (unlike bg_color, which several apps
# legitimately customise and which this family does not touch), so both are
# now computed fresh every window_draw call from color.border_strong and the
# legacy titlebar_active/titlebar_inactive keys (via WINDOW_FLAG_FOCUSED)
# instead of the cached fields. No value changes for this file; this is a
# staleness fix, not a recolour.
#
# --- window-decoration state tokens (#711 follow-up, designer 1 loop 1) ---
# window.c currently draws titlebar min/max/filter buttons in ONE state
# (rest) only, and the close button in two (rest/hover, via the legacy
# close_button/close_button_hover keys). These three keys give it the
# hover/active fills needed for the full six-state contract (focus is
# already expressed at the WINDOW level via the active/inactive titlebar
# swap, not per-button; disabled = button simply omitted, e.g. non-CLOSABLE
# windows already skip drawing the close button; selected does not apply
# to these momentary action buttons). Values are NOT new invented colours:
# btn_hover/btn_active are copied verbatim from the existing generic
# state.btn_hover_bg/state.btn_active_bg tokens above, and close_active is
# close_button mechanically darkened 15% (the same kind of deterministic
# derivation as the existing titlebar gradient's "lift 22% toward white",
# not an aesthetic pick).
# STATUS (#711 loop 2): WIRED for hover. kernel/gui/themes.h gained
# c_titlebar_btn_hover/active/close_active fields + TM_TITLE_INSET;
# kernel/gui/window.c's wm_handle_mouse_move now tracks which titlebar
# button (filter/min/max/close) the cursor is over per-window and
# window_draw fills that button with titlebar_btn_hover (close uses the
# pre-existing legacy close_button_hover key instead, its documented
# purpose, now also its first consumer). btn_active/close_active remain
# PARSED BUT NOT RENDERED: these three buttons execute their action on
# mouse DOWN with no frame boundary before the resulting redraw, so a
# "pressed" fill drawn synchronously before minimize/maximize/close runs
# is never actually presented - there is no timing system to hold it
# visible for even one frame (renderer limit, not a data gap). Rendering
# a real pressed state needs the buttons to execute on mouse UP instead
# (standard button semantics, matches every other click target in this
# renderer), which is a click-semantics change, not a chrome-drawing one;
# flagged to Substrate/engine as scoped follow-up, not done this loop.
color.titlebar_btn_hover=0x002E2E37
color.titlebar_btn_active=0x00191920
color.titlebar_close_active=0x00BE3B31
# Title text left inset from the border. WIRED (#711 loop 2): window.c reads
# metric.title_inset via TM_TITLE_INSET instead of the hardcoded
# "title_x = x + BORDER_WIDTH + 4"; unset themes default to 4 (pixel-identical
# to pre-loop-2). This is the modern spec value: 12px = space.md.
metric.title_inset=12

# --- space.* : the 4px spacing grid (surfaces family, loop 1, #711 follow-on) ---
# Six steps, xs..xxl. EVERY margin, padding and gap in EVERY brief must be one
# of these six numbers; no eyeballed pixel value. metric.pad above is space.md
# (12) and metric.gap is space.sm (8): those two are already read by the
# kernel offset table, so this scale is not new geometry, it is the NAMED
# scale those two values were already silently drawn from, now written down.
# The other four steps (xs, lg, xl, xxl) have no reader yet: nothing in
# kernel/gui/themes.c's g_theme_fields consumes "space.*" as a key. They are
# staged here as literal, forward-compatible data (per the mtheme v2 rule
# that unknown keys are ignored) so a family that needs a specific new
# metric.<widget>_pad has a numeric target to hit and a name to cite, not a
# blank page. Adding a reader for a NEW metric.* key that consumes one of
# these values is scoped engineering, not a data change; see
# docs/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md, "Surfaces & Foundation Spec".
space.xs=4
space.sm=8
space.md=12
space.lg=16
space.xl=24
space.xxl=32

# --- elevation.* : the four-level model, spelled out explicitly (surfaces
# family, loop 1). This is documentation of the existing color.* keys above,
# not new keys: the model IS a (surface token, border token) pair per level,
# there is nothing else to store. Written out here because "level 3 takes
# border_strong" was previously the only assignment on paper; levels 0-2 need
# stating too, since a family building a new surface has to pick one.
#   level 0 (sunken)  = color.surface_sunken  + color.border_subtle
#   level 1 (base)    = color.surface         + color.border_subtle
#   level 2 (raised)  = color.surface_raised  + color.border_subtle
#   level 3 (overlay) = color.surface_overlay + color.border_strong  (always)
# Dark scheme: higher level = lighter tint. sunken 0x121216 -> base 0x1A1A1F
# -> raised 0x24242B -> overlay 0x2C2C35, strictly increasing. No shadows:
# removed by #189, elevation is tint+border only, never a blur/soft-shadow.

# --- Taskbar / start menu / tray menu / notification: elevation assignment
# and geometry (surfaces family, loop 1). Colour is ALREADY theme-driven for
# these four (taskbar_bg/hover/active, menu_bg/border/item_hover, tooltip_bg
# are read today via the legacy THEME_* macros). GEOMETRY is not: taskbar.c,
# startmenu.c, traymenu.c and notif.c in userland/apps/compositor/ carry their
# own compiled #define heights/widths (TASKBAR_HEIGHT=36 in kernel/gui/
# desktop.h; LUMINA_MENUBAR_H=24/CLASSIC_UNIX_PANEL_H=58/RETRO_BENCH_BAR_H=20
# and TB_BTN_W=120/TRAY_ICON_W=26 in taskbar.c; TOAST_W=320/TOAST_H=64 in
# notif.c; TM_W=214/SND_W=308/SND_H=214 in traymenu.c). None of that is
# readable from a theme file today: editing a value here does nothing until
# an offset-table entry + a call-site read replaces the #define, exactly the
# "mechanism, not instance" pattern #711 already used for the five window
# chrome geometry macros. Flagging as scoped engineering, not claiming it
# live. Assignments and PROPOSED values (this file, on the surfaces scales):
#
#   Taskbar (persistent dock)   elevation 2 raised.  color.surface_raised +
#     color.border_subtle (top edge only, 1px, full-bleed: it never floats
#     free of the screen edge so an inset separator would read as a stray
#     line). PROPOSED metric.taskbar_h=44, derived from metric.btn_h(28) +
#     2*space.sm(8) so the bar has one space.sm of breathing room above and
#     below its own buttons, up from the compiled 36. Needs a
#     "retro ? <current per-style constant> : 44" default exactly like every
#     other metric.* field already follows, so the three alternate compiled
#     layouts (Lumina/Classic-Unix/Retro-Bench) keep their current heights
#     until a theme opts in by setting this key.
#   Start menu (launcher popup) elevation 3 overlay. color.surface_overlay +
#     color.border_strong, radius.menu, metric.menu_row_h per row. Reuses the
#     EXISTING generic menu metrics rather than inventing startmenu_* twins:
#     a start menu is structurally a menu. No new key needed.
#   Tray menu (volume/network/battery popups) elevation 3 overlay. Same
#     reuse: color.surface_overlay + color.border_strong, radius.menu,
#     metric.menu_row_h. No new key needed.
#   Notification toast   elevation 3 overlay. color.surface_overlay +
#     color.border_strong, radius.card (10, a self-contained card, not an
#     anchored menu, so the larger corner reads correctly). PROPOSED
#     metric.notif_w=320, metric.notif_h=64 (matches notif.c's current
#     compiled TOAST_W/TOAST_H exactly, so wiring this key changes nothing
#     visually until a theme edits it) and metric.notif_pad=space.md(12) for
#     its internal content inset.
metric.taskbar_h=44
metric.notif_w=320
metric.notif_h=64
metric.notif_pad=12

# --- Gradient policy check (surfaces family, loop 1): I own the POLICY
# (2-stop vertical only, subtle, max ~12% luminance delta, top stop always
# the lighter one, direction never inverts between schemes) but not the hex
# values in color.titlebar_top/bottom above (colour-scheme family's tokens).
# Measured this loop: titlebar_top 0x30303A -> titlebar_bottom 0x24242B is a
# 25.5% HSL-lightness swing (L 20.78 -> 15.49), over the ~12% budget; the
# maytera_light pair (0xFAFBFD -> 0xEDEFF3) measures 4.6%, comfortably inside
# it. Direction is correct in both (top lighter than bottom). Flagging the
# dark pair for the colour-scheme family to tighten; not editing the hex
# myself, see docs/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md.
#
# --- Resolved, Loop 2 (colour-scheme family): color.titlebar_top is now
# 0x26262F (was 0x30303A). 9.6% relative-luminance swing, 7.1% HSL-lightness
# swing against unchanged titlebar_bottom (0x24242B); both under the 12%
# budget. See the Loop 2 header block above (top of this file) for the full
# measurement, including why the Loop 1 CHANGELOG's "1.2%" figure for the
# old pair was wrong rather than a different, milder defect.

# --- #711 Loop 1 / Family 3 (Form Controls): checkbox, radio, dropdown,
# slider, text field, scrollbar --------------------------------------------
# Every value below is a LITERAL COPY of an already-defined color.*/state.*
# token above in THIS file (see the "# = tokenname" comment on each line).
# Nothing here is an invented hex; only the metric.*/radius.* geometry keys
# are genuinely new, and they are additive to the existing scale, never a
# redefinition of a shared token. All six states (rest/hover/active/focus/
# disabled/selected) are covered per control; where a state does not apply
# to a given control it is documented as N/A with its fallback, never left
# to be guessed via alpha (this renderer has no per-pixel alpha).
#
# STATUS (#711 loop2, verified against the running source, not assumed):
# every metric./radius./state.field_*/state.radio_*/state.slider_*/
# state.scrollbar_* key below (38 keys) has ZERO consumers today. Checked
# all three checkbox draw paths that exist in this codebase
# (kernel/gui/widget.c widget_draw_checkbox, userland/libc/gui.c's legacy
# gui_draw_checkbox, and userland/libc/gui.c's modern gui_checkbox via
# gui_style.h) plus gui_slider/gui_textfield2/gui_toggle and every app that
# hand-rolls radio/dropdown/scrollbar: none read a control-specific key from
# this section, and the modern gui_checkbox/gui_slider/gui_textfield2 do not
# even read the generic state.input_* keys - they read a separate, smaller
# gui_palette_t struct (10 fields, no per-state slots) that most apps
# populate from a PRIVATE hardcoded 12-theme colour table in their own
# source, not from SYS_THEME_COLOR/SYS_THEME_METRIC at all. This is a
# deeper gap than "the offset table has no row for this key" (#711 loop1's
# framing): the consumer-side DATA MODEL these controls actually draw from
# has no room for most of these tokens yet. Full finding, three-
# implementation inventory, and a concrete minimal wiring spec (reusing
# already-shipped primitives, not inventing new renderer capability) are in
# docs/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md, "Family 3 (Form Controls), Loop 2". Kept in place
# rather than deleted per this loop's B11 instruction ("moved to a clearly
# marked DEFERRED section... or deleted, with the count recorded") - this
# IS that marking. Not deleted because the geometry values (checkbox_box,
# radio_d/dot/ring_w, dropdown_row_h, slider_*, scrollbar_thumb_min) and the
# newly-fixed state.input_rest_border contrast (below) are correct, reusable
# targets for whichever engineering pass wires them, and re-deriving them
# from scratch would waste the contrast/geometry work already checked here.

# TEXT FIELD. Rest/hover/active/focus/disabled/selected reuse state.input_*
# verbatim (already defined above): rest_bg/rest_fg/rest_border, hover_border,
# active_border, focus_ring, disabled_bg/disabled_fg, selected_bg/selected_fg
# (selected = the in-field text-selection highlight). Only the modifier
# states below are new: an invalid field additionally shows a danger border
# on top of whatever base state it is in; caret and placeholder are fixed
# regardless of state.
state.field_invalid_border=0x00D93A2F     # = color.danger
state.field_placeholder_fg=0x009AA0AA     # #711 loop3 (Family 3): repointed to the new
                                           # dedicated color.placeholder token (director
                                           # loop-3 shared decision, section 1) instead of
                                           # aliasing color.on_surface_muted (0x9AA3AF,
                                           # still used elsewhere) - close but distinct per
                                           # spec. 7.10:1 on state.input_rest_bg (0x121216),
                                           # measured (WCAG relative luminance).
state.field_caret=0x002563C9              # = color.accent

# CHECKBOX. Box states reuse state.input_* verbatim: rest_bg/rest_border,
# hover_border, active_border, focus_ring, disabled_bg/disabled_fg. Checked
# ("selected") reuses state.input_selected_bg for the box fill and
# state.input_selected_fg for the check glyph - both already defined above,
# so checkbox needs zero new color keys, only geometry:
metric.checkbox_box=16
radius.control=4                          # #711 loop2: was 3, B6 settled value (buttons/
                                           # inputs/dropdowns/menus/selection rows all = 4px)
# Check-glyph pixel spec (16x16 box, local coords, origin = box top-left):
# the renderer has no public AA line, so this is a stepped 2px polyline
# built from eleven 2x2 filled rects (state.input_selected_fg colour),
# Bresenham-plotted from (3,7) through the pivot (6,10) to (12,3):
#   (3,7) (4,8) (5,9) (6,10)                          <- short leg, down-right
#   (6,10) (7,9) (8,8) (9,7) (9,6) (10,5) (11,4) (12,3) <- long leg, up-right
# (6,10) is shared by both legs. This is a consumer-code spec, not a new
# .mtheme key: see report for why the glyph shape stays a compiled constant
# while every colour/size around it is data.

# RADIO. Ring on background reuses state.input_rest_bg (no new bg key). Ring
# stroke and dot are new: a 14px outer diameter is the renderer's hard-edge
# Bresenham-circle floor (below it a filled circle looks ragged, not a taste
# choice), with a 2px ring stroke via nested-fill (outer circle filled in
# the ring colour, inner circle inset 2px filled in state.radio_bg) and a
# 6px filled dot when selected, per the exact brief spec.
metric.radio_d=14
metric.radio_dot=6
metric.radio_ring_w=2
state.radio_bg=0x00121216                 # = state.input_rest_bg
state.radio_ring_rest=0x009AA3AF          # = state.input_rest_border (loop2 fix, see above)
state.radio_ring_hover=0x005D636C         # = state.input_hover_border
state.radio_ring_active=0x001B4FA6        # = color.accent_active
state.radio_ring_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF    # = color.focus_ring
state.radio_ring_disabled=0x005D636C      # = color.on_surface_disabled
state.radio_ring_selected=0x002563C9      # = color.accent
state.radio_dot_selected=0x002563C9       # = color.accent
state.radio_dot_disabled=0x005D636C       # = color.on_surface_disabled

# DROPDOWN / COMBOBOX. Closed box reuses state.input_* verbatim (rest/hover/
# active/focus/disabled); "selected" does not apply to the closed box itself
# (no checked concept) and falls back to rest. Popup rows reuse state.item_*
# verbatim (rest/hover/active/focus/disabled/selected = the current choice).
# Popup surface is the level-3 overlay pair already owned by family 6:
# color.surface_overlay (0x002C2C35) + color.border_strong (0x00454551) -
# consumed here, not redefined. Only the roomier form-control row height is
# new (window/system menus keep the existing metric.menu_row_h=28):
metric.dropdown_row_h=32

# SLIDER. Track is a static 4px groove (one colour, no hover/press of its
# own - dragging anywhere on the track hands off straight to the thumb's
# active state, so a track has no separate 6-state set; documented, not
# guessed). The accent-filled thumb carries the six states; "selected" is
# N/A (a slider is continuous, not a discrete choice) and aliases to rest.
metric.slider_track_h=4
metric.slider_thumb_w=12
metric.slider_thumb_h=18
radius.slider_thumb=3
state.slider_track=0x00121216             # = color.surface_sunken
state.slider_fill=0x002563C9              # = color.accent
state.slider_fill_disabled=0x005D636C     # = color.on_surface_disabled
state.slider_thumb_rest=0x002563C9        # = color.accent
state.slider_thumb_hover=0x003170D8       # = color.accent_hover
state.slider_thumb_active=0x001B4FA6      # = color.accent_active
state.slider_thumb_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF  # = color.focus_ring
state.slider_thumb_disabled=0x005D636C    # = color.on_surface_disabled

# SCROLLBAR. Track is a static groove, same reasoning as the slider's.
# Thumb carries the six states; "selected" is N/A (no discrete-choice
# concept) and aliases to active (dragging is the only extra thumb state).
# Minimum thumb length is a hard usability floor, not a style choice.
metric.scrollbar_thumb_min=24
radius.scrollbar_thumb=8                  # = metric.scrollbar_w / 2 (pill cap)
state.scrollbar_track=0x00121216          # = color.surface_sunken
state.scrollbar_thumb_rest=0x00454551     # = color.border_strong
state.scrollbar_thumb_hover=0x009AA3AF    # = color.on_surface_muted
state.scrollbar_thumb_active=0x002563C9   # = color.accent
state.scrollbar_thumb_focus_ring=0x004C8EFF # = color.focus_ring
state.scrollbar_thumb_disabled=0x002E2E37 # = color.border_subtle

# =====================================================================
# DEFERRED (Loop 2, per director B11 "token surface diet"): everything
# from here to end of file is the Loop 1 six-variant button matrix
# (standard/primary/danger/toolbar/pill/segmented x six states x
# fill/text/border). Verified this loop via grep against
# kernel/gui/themes.c: g_theme_fields[] has NO entry for any btn.<variant>
# key, so all of it is currently dead data - 0 consumers, exactly the
# defect the director's redirect measured ("103 btn.* keys ... grep
# returns 0 hits"). Rather than delete a loop of real design work
# outright, it is kept here, clearly marked, for whichever future loop
# wires per-variant button drawing. It is NOT current spec: two things
# below are superseded by Loop 2's settled shared decisions and must be
# corrected before this block is ever revived as live data:
#   1. B6 ("No pills") retires full pill rounding outright. radius.pill
#      is deleted (was 14 = metric.btn_h/2). When revived, btn.pill and
#      btn.segmented's outer corners take the unified control radius
#      instead (see the shared-token ask in the report - B6 names
#      radius.control=4 for this and the parser has no such field yet).
#   2. radius.btn_toolbar (was 3, a per-variant rung) is deleted for the
#      same reason: one shared control radius, not one per button shape.
# The live, wired button styling for THIS loop is the minimal generic
# matrix above (state.btn_* including the four new *_border keys and
# the selected_focus_ring fix) - see the docs/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md Buttons
# section, "Loop 2 update", for the full account.
# =====================================================================
# --- #711 Loop 1 / Family 2 (Buttons): standard, primary, danger, toolbar/
# icon, pill, segmented control ---------------------------------------------
# Every colour below is a LITERAL COPY of an already-defined color.*/state.*
# token above in THIS file (see the "# = tokenname" comment on each line,
# same convention Family 3 used for checkbox/radio/slider/scrollbar). Nothing
# here is an invented hex; only metric.btn_pad_h/btn_pad_icon and
# radius.btn_toolbar are genuinely new geometry, additive to the existing
# scale, never a redefinition of a shared token. type.body_strong and
# radius.pill are NOT this family's tokens (typography/radius scale, see the
# note at the end of this block) but are consumed here since Family 2 is the
# first real consumer of both.
#
# Geometry (space.lg/space.sm from the surfaces family's grid above):
metric.btn_pad_h=16                       # = space.lg, horizontal button padding (deferred)
metric.btn_pad_icon=4                     # = space.xs, icon-only toolbar button padding (deferred)
metric.btn_gap=8                          # = space.sm, gap between grouped buttons (same
                                           #   value as metric.gap; deferred with the rest)
# radius.btn_toolbar (was 3, a per-variant rung) DELETED this loop (Loop 2,
# B6): one shared control radius, not one per button shape - see the
# DEFERRED header above.
#
# NESTED-FILL TECHNIQUE (every variant below uses it): the renderer has no
# stroked/outlined rounded rect. Each state's ".border" value is the OUTER
# fill, drawn first; ".bg" is the INNER fill, inset by metric.border_w (1px)
# on every edge. Where ".border" equals ".bg" the button reads as flush/
# borderless by construction, not as a special case the renderer needs to
# know about. Focus is separate: btn.<variant>.focus.ring is drawn as a
# metric.focus_w (2px) rect OUTLINE offset 2px OUTSIDE the button's own
# bounds - square corners even on the pill/segmented variants, per the
# renderer's no-AA-rounded-outline limit (explicitly allowed by the brief).
#
# STANDARD (secondary) push button. Mirrors the existing generic state.btn_*
# pair-for-pair (identical literals) and adds the border tokens that
# primitive did not carry.
btn.standard.rest.bg=0x0024242B           # = state.btn_rest_bg
btn.standard.rest.fg=0x00F2F3F5           # = state.btn_rest_fg
btn.standard.rest.border=0x002E2E37       # = color.border_subtle
btn.standard.hover.bg=0x002E2E37          # = state.btn_hover_bg
btn.standard.hover.fg=0x00F2F3F5          # = state.btn_hover_fg
btn.standard.hover.border=0x002E2E37      # = color.border_subtle (unchanged; fill lifts)
btn.standard.active.bg=0x00191920         # = state.btn_active_bg (distinct pressed fill,
                                           #   not an alpha trick)
btn.standard.active.fg=0x00F2F3F5         # = state.btn_active_fg
btn.standard.active.border=0x00454551     # = color.border_strong (deepens on press)
btn.standard.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF        # = color.focus_ring
btn.standard.disabled.bg=0x0024242B       # = state.btn_disabled_bg
btn.standard.disabled.fg=0x005D636C       # = state.btn_disabled_fg
btn.standard.disabled.border=0x002E2E37   # = color.border_subtle
btn.standard.selected.bg=0x002563C9       # = state.btn_selected_bg
btn.standard.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF       # = state.btn_selected_fg
btn.standard.selected.border=0x002563C9   # = color.accent (flush)
#
# PRIMARY / default button (accent-filled). Label uses type.body_strong, not
# type.body (see typography note below). Disabled DESATURATES to the same
# neutral treatment as a disabled standard button rather than dimming the
# accent - a dimmed-but-still-blue button can misread as still actionable.
btn.primary.rest.bg=0x002563C9            # = color.accent
btn.primary.rest.fg=0x00FFFFFF            # = color.on_accent
btn.primary.rest.border=0x001B4FA6        # = color.accent_active (crisp 1px edge so a
                                           #   filled button still reads as bounded)
btn.primary.hover.bg=0x003170D8           # = color.accent_hover
btn.primary.hover.fg=0x00FFFFFF           # = color.on_accent
btn.primary.hover.border=0x001B4FA6       # = color.accent_active
btn.primary.active.bg=0x001B4FA6          # = color.accent_active (distinct pressed fill)
btn.primary.active.fg=0x00FFFFFF          # = color.on_accent
btn.primary.active.border=0x001B4FA6      # = color.accent_active
btn.primary.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF         # = color.focus_ring
btn.primary.disabled.bg=0x0024242B        # = color.surface_raised (neutral, not dim accent)
btn.primary.disabled.fg=0x005D636C        # = color.on_surface_disabled
btn.primary.disabled.border=0x002E2E37    # = color.border_subtle
btn.primary.selected.bg=0x001B4FA6        # = color.accent_active (rare: a primary-styled
                                           #   toggle, e.g. a single big filled action that
                                           #   stays "on"; most primaries never hit this)
btn.primary.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF        # = color.on_accent
btn.primary.selected.border=0x001B4FA6    # = color.accent_active
#
# DANGER button. color.danger/on_danger measures 4.58:1 in THIS file as of
# this loop (Family 4 deepened color.danger 0xFF453A -> 0xD93A2F this same
# loop specifically to clear WCAG AA - see that family's comment above and
# the report). Fill therefore stays the constant, AA-passing color.danger at
# every interactive state (there is no color.danger_hover/danger_active
# token yet, and hexes are not this family's to invent - see report); hover
# and pressed are still genuinely distinct, non-alpha tokens because the
# OUTER nested-fill ring moves instead: flush at rest, a dark ring on hover,
# a bright ring on press. All three read as clearly different states.
btn.danger.rest.bg=0x00D93A2F             # = color.danger
btn.danger.rest.fg=0x00FFFFFF             # = color.on_danger
btn.danger.rest.border=0x00D93A2F         # = color.danger (flush)
btn.danger.hover.bg=0x00D93A2F            # = color.danger
btn.danger.hover.fg=0x00FFFFFF            # = color.on_danger
btn.danger.hover.border=0x00454551        # = color.border_strong (dark defining ring)
btn.danger.active.bg=0x00D93A2F           # = color.danger
btn.danger.active.fg=0x00FFFFFF           # = color.on_danger
btn.danger.active.border=0x00FFFFFF       # = color.on_danger (bright ring, distinct
                                           #   token from both rest's and hover's border)
btn.danger.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF          # = color.focus_ring (same ring colour as every
                                           #   other control - focus must read as ONE signal
                                           #   regardless of the control's own semantic hue)
btn.danger.disabled.bg=0x0024242B         # = color.surface_raised (neutral, not dim red)
btn.danger.disabled.fg=0x005D636C         # = color.on_surface_disabled
btn.danger.disabled.border=0x002E2E37     # = color.border_subtle
btn.danger.selected.bg=0x00D93A2F         # = color.danger (rare: momentary-action buttons
                                           #   are not usually toggled)
btn.danger.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF         # = color.on_danger
btn.danger.selected.border=0x00FFFFFF     # = color.on_danger
#
# TOOLBAR / ICON button. Flush with the ambient surface at rest (no visible
# chrome until interacted with), lifts on hover, and on press SINKS to the
# elevation-0 sunken pair - reusing the elevation model literally instead of
# inventing a fourth colour: pressed = sunken.
btn.toolbar.rest.bg=0x001A1A1F            # = color.surface
btn.toolbar.rest.fg=0x00F2F3F5            # = color.on_surface
btn.toolbar.rest.border=0x001A1A1F        # = color.surface (flush)
btn.toolbar.hover.bg=0x0024242B           # = color.surface_raised
btn.toolbar.hover.fg=0x00F2F3F5           # = color.on_surface
btn.toolbar.hover.border=0x002E2E37       # = color.border_subtle
btn.toolbar.active.bg=0x00121216          # = color.surface_sunken (elevation 0: pressed)
btn.toolbar.active.fg=0x00F2F3F5          # = color.on_surface
btn.toolbar.active.border=0x00454551      # = color.border_strong
btn.toolbar.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF         # = color.focus_ring
btn.toolbar.disabled.bg=0x001A1A1F        # = color.surface (stays flush)
btn.toolbar.disabled.fg=0x005D636C        # = color.on_surface_disabled
btn.toolbar.disabled.border=0x001A1A1F    # = color.surface
btn.toolbar.selected.bg=0x002563C9        # = color.accent (toggled-on icon button, e.g.
                                           #   Bold/Bookmark)
btn.toolbar.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF        # = color.on_accent
btn.toolbar.selected.border=0x001B4FA6    # = color.accent_active
#
# PILL button (filter chip). Rounded fully via radius.pill (below); colour
# behaviour otherwise matches the standard button's rest/hover/active so a
# row of pills and a row of standard buttons read as the same material.
btn.pill.rest.bg=0x0024242B               # = state.btn_rest_bg
btn.pill.rest.fg=0x00F2F3F5               # = state.btn_rest_fg
btn.pill.rest.border=0x002E2E37           # = color.border_subtle
btn.pill.hover.bg=0x002E2E37              # = state.btn_hover_bg
btn.pill.hover.fg=0x00F2F3F5              # = state.btn_hover_fg
btn.pill.hover.border=0x002E2E37          # = color.border_subtle
btn.pill.active.bg=0x00191920             # = state.btn_active_bg
btn.pill.active.fg=0x00F2F3F5             # = state.btn_active_fg
btn.pill.active.border=0x00454551         # = color.border_strong
btn.pill.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF            # = color.focus_ring
btn.pill.disabled.bg=0x0024242B           # = state.btn_disabled_bg
btn.pill.disabled.fg=0x005D636C           # = state.btn_disabled_fg
btn.pill.disabled.border=0x002E2E37       # = color.border_subtle
btn.pill.selected.bg=0x002563C9           # = color.accent (chip becomes solid when chosen)
btn.pill.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF           # = color.on_accent
btn.pill.selected.border=0x001B4FA6       # = color.accent_active
#
# SEGMENTED CONTROL. One connected group sharing radius.btn (6) on the outer
# ends only; internal joins are flat, divided by a metric.border_w (1px)
# line in btn.segmented.divider. Unselected segments use muted ink so the
# selected segment reads first without needing a second colour signal.
# selected_hover/selected_active cover re-interacting with the segment that
# is already chosen; disabled_selected is a second disabled colour because
# in dark scheme color.surface_overlay is distinct from color.surface_raised
# (unlike the light scheme - see maytera_light.mtheme's note on this line),
# so a disabled control can still show which segment was selected.
btn.segmented.rest.bg=0x0024242B          # = color.surface_raised (unselected)
btn.segmented.rest.fg=0x009AA3AF          # = color.on_surface_muted
btn.segmented.rest.border=0x0024242B      # = color.surface_raised (flush; divider is
                                           #   the visible separator, not this border)
btn.segmented.hover.bg=0x002E2E37         # = state.btn_hover_bg
btn.segmented.hover.fg=0x00F2F3F5         # = color.on_surface
btn.segmented.hover.border=0x002E2E37     # = state.btn_hover_bg
btn.segmented.active.bg=0x00191920        # = state.btn_active_bg
btn.segmented.active.fg=0x00F2F3F5        # = color.on_surface
btn.segmented.active.border=0x00191920    # = state.btn_active_bg
btn.segmented.focus.ring=0x004C8EFF       # = color.focus_ring (drawn around the one
                                           #   keyboard-focused segment, not the group)
btn.segmented.disabled.bg=0x0024242B      # = color.surface_raised (unselected, disabled)
btn.segmented.disabled.fg=0x005D636C      # = color.on_surface_disabled
btn.segmented.disabled.border=0x0024242B  # = color.surface_raised
btn.segmented.selected.bg=0x002563C9      # = color.accent
btn.segmented.selected.fg=0x00FFFFFF      # = color.on_accent
btn.segmented.selected.border=0x002563C9  # = color.accent
btn.segmented.selected_hover.bg=0x003170D8    # = color.accent_hover
btn.segmented.selected_hover.fg=0x00FFFFFF    # = color.on_accent
btn.segmented.selected_active.bg=0x001B4FA6   # = color.accent_active
btn.segmented.selected_active.fg=0x00FFFFFF   # = color.on_accent
btn.segmented.disabled_selected.bg=0x002C2C35 # = color.surface_overlay (distinct from
                                               #   plain disabled.bg so the disabled group
                                               #   still shows which segment was chosen)
btn.segmented.disabled_selected.fg=0x005D636C # = color.on_surface_disabled
btn.segmented.divider=0x002E2E37          # = color.border_subtle, drawn at metric.border_w
#
# Shared-scale tokens this family consumes but does not own. Neither line
# below changes an existing value; both fill a gap this family hit first.
# type.body_strong: the typography family's own #711-follow-on doc section
# states it is "present in all 14 shipped .mtheme files (added this loop)"
# and lists 14px/20px/bold - true for the 12 pre-#711 themes, NOT yet true
# for maytera_dark/maytera_light as of this read. Synced here at the
# IDENTICAL value already established elsewhere (not invented by this
# family) because the primary button's label needs it; flagged to the
# typography family as a two-file sync gap, see report.
type.body_strong=14
type.body_strong_lineheight=20
type.body_strong_weight=bold
# radius.pill: the brief's own radius scale names five rungs (none/sm/md/
# lg/pill) but only four (none/sm/md/lg, realized as radius.btn/input/menu/
# card=6/6/6/10) had been instantiated as a key anywhere before this loop.
# 14 = metric.btn_h/2 at the modern default (28/2), the formula the scale
# itself specifies. This family is the first pill-shaped consumer; flagged
# to the radius-scale owner to confirm/adopt so a future chip/tag control
# elsewhere reuses this key instead of a second one, see report.

# --- #711 Loop 2 / Family: surfaces (wiring + repair, director redirect) ---
# This loop is explicitly NOT polish: Section A of the redirect calls Loop 2
# "a WIRING AND REPAIR loop" and Section F says verify the artifact, not the
# diff. Everything below is either (a) a value change to an ALREADY-WIRED
# key (real, live, no engineering needed - see the radius.btn/input/menu and
# radius.control edits made in place above, not here) or (b) new data with
# its wiring status stated explicitly, never implied.
#
# B6 (radius) and B9 (focus ring) are handled as in-place edits above, next
# to the keys they change, not duplicated here - a second, later definition
# of the same key would be ambiguous for this line-reader parser (see the
# blame.md entry this loop adds on exactly that risk).
#
# B7 (spacing): "8px base grid... Settings section gap 24, intra-section
# gap 8" needs NO new key. space.xl=24 and space.sm=8 already exist above
# (surfaces, loop 1) and are exactly those two values. Settings family
# consumes them directly; nothing for this family to add.
#
# B8 (elevation: shadow bands, scrim, inactive titlebar) - the one item
# this loop had to actually investigate rather than restate, because my own
# loop-1 brief says "NO SHADOWS EVER... unrenderable" and the task's own
# renderer-limits section bans "drop shadows of ANY kind" and "full-screen
# translucent scrims", which reads as a flat contradiction of B8. Read the
# actual draw code before deciding which instruction was stale:
#
#  1. MENU/POPUP EDGE BAND - RENDERABLE, PROVEN, ALREADY SHIPPING.
#     kernel/gui/window.c's wm_draw_winmenu() (the window-decorator popup)
#     already does exactly what B8 asks, today, in a released build:
#       fb_fill_rect(x+3, y+3, w, h, 0x00101418);   // offset dark rect first
#       fb_fill_rect(x,   y,   w, h, p.bg);          // popup body on top
#       fb_draw_rect(x,   y,   w, h, p.border);      // 1px border
#     This is a hard-edged OPAQUE offset rect, not alpha - "shadow" here
#     means "solid dark rect peeking out 3px on the right/bottom before the
#     surface itself is drawn on top", never a blur. Zero new primitives:
#     fb_fill_rect is called everywhere. My loop-1 "no shadows" language was
#     about soft/blurred drop shadows specifically (the #189 removal), not
#     this hard silhouette technique, which #189 never touched.
#  2. userland/apps/compositor/notif.c goes one step further and proves the
#     SOFT version is also fine at small scale: its toast shadow uses the
#     compositor's uniform per-draw alpha (g_draw_blend=70 of 255, ~27%,
#     within a hair of B8's stated 25%) on an offset rounded rect, exactly
#     the "uniform-opacity dim... allowed ONLY for small regions" exception
#     my own brief already carves out. Two independent, already-shipping
#     implementations of the same idea; this is not a new ask.
#  3. FULL-SCREEN 30% SCRIM BEHIND A MODAL - NOT IMPLEMENTED, FLAGGING A
#     REAL ARCHITECTURE QUESTION, not refusing on principle. The primitive
#     exists (kernel fb_fill_rect_alpha, proven at full-screen size by
#     kernel/gui/login.c's LOGIN_SCRIM_COLOR/LOGIN_SCRIM_ALPHA=145). The
#     reason my brief bans it is NOT "the renderer can't draw a translucent
#     rect", it is the cost of REDRAWING one every frame: login.c's own
#     comment says "blit plus a scrim is too expensive for a per-frame
#     redraw, #426", and it gets away with it because the login screen is
#     effectively static underneath. A modal opened over the live desktop
#     is a different case ONLY IF the compositor keeps repainting whatever
#     is under the modal while it is open (clock tick, taskbar gauges,
#     other windows). If a modal already suspends redraw of everything
#     below it (which "modal blocks input to lower windows" implies but I
#     have not verified in the compositor's damage-rect code, and it is not
#     mine to verify or change), the scrim is a ONE-TIME full-screen draw
#     at modal-open, not a per-frame cost, and my blanket ban is too strong
#     for this specific case. THIS IS AN ENGINEERING QUESTION FOR THE
#     SUBSTRATE PHASE, not a design decision: does the compositor freeze
#     repaint of everything below an open modal? I am not implementing the
#     scrim until that is answered, because implementing it wrong silently
#     reintroduces the exact input-stall class bug #426 already paid for.
#     Modal dialogs DO get the edge-band technique from item 1 above at
#     double width (6px, matching B8's modal figure) - that part is exactly
#     as renderable as the menu case, only the full-screen scrim is gated.
#
#  Tokens for the edge-band technique (new, this loop; width/alpha only -
#  the colour is pure black, which is not a per-theme choice the way a
#  surface tint is, so it is the same literal in every theme file rather
#  than something for the colour-scheme family to pick per scheme):
color.elevation_edge=0x00000000
metric.elevation_edge_w_menu=3            # popup/menu/dropdown/tray/notif band
metric.elevation_edge_w_modal=6           # modal dialog band (double width)
metric.elevation_edge_alpha_menu=64       # ~25% of 255, matches B8's figure
metric.elevation_edge_alpha_modal=89      # ~35% of 255, matches B8's figure
# Consumer guidance (not a new key, a usage note): a kernel-side draw site
# (window.c) uses the OPAQUE technique already proven in wm_draw_winmenu -
# fb_fill_rect(x+w_edge, y+w_edge, w, h, <a near-black solid, e.g. the
# existing 0x00101418 literal - promoting THAT literal to a token is a
# one-line follow-up, not done here to avoid touching a call site this
# family does not own>) - because the kernel's fb_fill_rect_alpha path is
# reserved for login.c's one-shot full-screen case per item 3 above, and a
# per-popup alpha call is unnecessary complexity when the opaque technique
# already reads correctly (proven, on screen, today). A compositor-side
# draw site (userland: start menu, tray menu) should prefer the ALREADY
# WIRED g_draw_blend technique from notif.c, using
# metric.elevation_edge_alpha_menu as the g_draw_blend value and
# color.elevation_edge as the fill colour, at metric.elevation_edge_w_menu
# offset. Scoped engineering either way: applying an existing, proven
# technique to more call sites, not inventing a primitive.
#
#  INACTIVE TITLEBAR (B8's other half). NOT this family's tokens to hex -
#  color.titlebar_text_inactive/titlebar_inactive_top/titlebar_inactive_bottom
#  belong to the window-decorations family's chrome-token group (they
#  already did a real AA pass on titlebar_text_inactive in loop 1, commit
#  a8e1bec: 3.23:1 -> 5.11:1+). B8 asks for dark = literal #1B1B20; this
#  file's CURRENT titlebar_inactive_bottom is 0x001A1A1F (a 3-value, <1%
#  luminance difference from #1B1B20 - visually indistinguishable, almost
#  certainly the same design intent already realized under a slightly
#  different literal) and titlebar_inactive_top is 0x001F1F25. I am NOT
#  editing these: they are outside my token ownership (colour hexes) and
#  outside my family's file section (window-decorations' territory).
#  Flagging the near-match for that family/the director to confirm rather
#  than silently declaring B8 "already done" or duplicating the tokens here.
#
# radius.pill / "no pills" (B6) - CONFLICT WITH ANOTHER FAMILY'S UNCOMMITTED
# WORK, not resolved by me. radius.pill=14 and a full btn.pill.* six-state
# block exist further up this file (Family 2/buttons, loop 1, uncommitted
# as of this loop - see `git status`). B6 states "No pills" for Loop 2.
# radius.pill is a radius-SCALE token, which is this family's to own, but
# the btn.pill.* colour/state block that CONSUMES it belongs to Family 2 and
# is not mine to edit or delete ("do not edit another designer's family").
# I am leaving both as-is and flagging: per B6, no button in this loop
# should render as a pill, which means either Family 2's pill variant is
# withdrawn from Loop 2 scope, or B6 needs to carve out an explicit
# exception for it - the director/Family 2 need to reconcile this, not me.
# radius.pill itself is NOT deprecated as a scale rung: radius.
# scrollbar_thumb=8 (Family 3, loop 1, already in this file) is the same
# "pill cap" concept applied to a scrollbar thumb, which is not a button and
# not covered by B6's "no pills" (that clause is specifically about button
# shape). The rung stays; only pill-shaped BUTTONS are out for this loop.
#
# Separator spec (surfaces family, restated - unchanged since loop 1, no
# edit needed): 1px, color.border_subtle, full-bleed for a separator that
# touches a container's own edge (e.g. the taskbar's top edge, already
# spec'd above), inset by space.lg (16) on both ends for a separator that
# floats inside a panel's content area (e.g. between two Settings sections
# - NOT the B7 24px section gap itself, which is whitespace, not a rule).
# No new token: this uses color.border_subtle and metric.border_w (1),
# both already defined and wired.

# --- #711 Loop 2 / Family 2 (Buttons): reply to the surfaces family's
# radius.pill flag above -----------------------------------------------
# Resolved, not left open. Per B6 ("No pills", settled, no re-deciding):
# radius.pill=14 is DELETED from this file (it is no longer defined
# anywhere below - only mentioned in the deferred block's prose, which
# describes Loop 1 history, not current state). The btn.pill.* six-state
# block is not withdrawn or deleted; it is moved into the single DEFERRED
# block above (with the rest of the six-variant matrix, 0 consumers either
# way per kernel/gui/themes.c) and annotated there that when it is
# eventually revived, it takes the unified control radius instead of a
# full round. No exception carve-out needed: there is currently no
# pill-shaped BUTTON rendering on any booted system (the matrix has zero
# consumers), so B6 is not in tension with anything actually on screen
# this loop. radius.scrollbar_thumb=8 (Family 3's scrollbar-thumb pill
# cap) is untouched, matching the surfaces family's own read that B6's
# "no pills" is about button shape, not every rounded-cap use of the
# radius scale.

# --- #711 Loop 3 / Family 3 (Form Controls): director shared-token decisions
# (section 1) that land in this family's territory ---------------------------
# color.placeholder: a NEW, dedicated token for text-field placeholder ink,
# distinct from color.on_surface_muted even though the two are close in this
# scheme (0x9AA0AA vs 0x9AA3AF) - per the director's explicit instruction,
# not my own invention. state.field_placeholder_fg above now points at this
# token, not at on_surface_muted (see the edit note on that line). 7.10:1 on
# state.input_rest_bg (0x121216), comfortably clears WCAG AA 4.5:1 even
# though placeholder text is exempt from the requirement (it is example
# text, not real content), and clears it in both themes for margin.
color.placeholder=0x009AA0AA

# radius.selection: the corner radius for the shared selected-row treatment
# (director section 1: "solid accent fill, white text, radius 4, inset 4px",
# converging Start menu / Settings sidebar / Files sidebar / dialog lists /
# THIS family's dropdown popup selected row onto one look). Same numeric
# value as the already-wired radius.control (4) - given its own name because
# the director's spec names it as a distinct concept from control-box
# rounding, even though on this scale they currently coincide. Consumed by
# my dropdown popup's selected-row highlight; other consumers belong to
# their owning families (Shell/Settings/Files per the director's redirect).
radius.selection=4
# metric.selection_inset: the 4px inset from a selected row's own left/right
# edges the accent fill sits at, per the same director spec. = space.xs (4).
# New key, additive, zero risk to any existing consumer.
metric.selection_inset=4

# --- #711 Loop 3 / Family 4 (colour schemes): director shared-token
# decisions (section 1) that land in THIS family's territory ---------------
# color.placeholder above (0x9AA0AA) was already added, correctly, by
# Family 3 (Form Controls) while this pass was in flight - see that
# family's comment further up. Noted for the record, not re-added: two
# concurrent processes independently derived the identical literal from
# the same director spec, so there is no value disagreement, only an
# ownership one (my own brief: 'you are the ONLY designer who writes hex
# values ... in exactly two files'). Flagging to the director rather than
# fighting the working tree over it - see report. I did NOT re-add or
# duplicate the key: this parser has no defined behaviour for two
# definitions of the same key in one file, and a second, later assignment
# would be genuinely ambiguous, not just untidy (this file's own Loop 2
# history already carries the same warning on radius.btn/input/menu).
#
# color.selection_bg / color.selection_fg (director's own naming for this
# ask) were NOT added as new keys either. The canonical token model this
# substrate defines already names this exact pair color.sel_bg/
# color.sel_fg (both already present above, unchanged since Loop 1: 
# 0x2563C9 / 0xFFFFFF, i.e. color.accent / color.on_accent, 5.67:1).
# Adding a second key meaning the same thing would be the duplicate-token
# anti-pattern Loop 2's B11 'token surface diet' and the Files-sidebar
# 'three colours for one state' defect both exist to retire. Consumers
# (Shell/Settings/Files/Dialogs selected-row work) should read
# color.sel_bg/color.sel_fg; radius 4 is the already-live radius.control
# (or the new radius.selection Family 3 added alongside color.placeholder,
# same value, not mine to arbitrate which name wins - flagged to director);
# the 4px inset is geometry (metric.selection_inset, also already added),
# not a colour token.
#
# Two genuinely NEW colour tokens below, values as specified verbatim by
# the director (section 1), contrast measured with the same WCAG relative-
# luminance method as every ratio elsewhere in this file (scripted, not
# eyeballed):
#  - color.section_header: promotes the RGB(102,187,102)/0x66BB66 constant
#    that Settings currently hardcodes per-file to a real token, so the
#    single literal has one source of truth instead of N copies.
#    7.32:1 against color.surface (0x1A1A1F), 6.30:1 against
#    color.surface_raised (0x24242B). Director's own figure for this hue
#    was '6.4 to 7.3:1 (keep)'; this file measures at the top of that
#    band, still a clear pass either way.
#  - color.text_muted: dedicated token for Settings-style field-caption
#    text ('Font Size', 'Icon Size', 'Set time automatically'), the
#    B3-era sub-3:1-under-lint.baseline=legacy-v1 defect the director
#    revoked the exemption for. THIS file already had no such defect
#    (color.on_surface_muted here measures 6.79:1, see the Loop 2 header
#    above) - the defect the director is retiring lives in the 12 LEGACY
#    theme files, not this one, which is outside my mandate to touch.
#    color.text_muted is added anyway because the director's instruction
#    is unconditional ('no designer may deviate'), and because it gives
#    Settings/F3 a role-specific name distinct from color.on_surface_muted
#    (the elevation-model/dropdown/disabled-text token) even though the
#    two literals are close by construction - both are the same neutral
#    ramp near this scheme's AA floor, they are just not the same design
#    decision or the same consumer. 7.08:1 against color.surface, 6.30:1
#    against color.surface_raised - clears the 4.5:1 AA BODY-text floor,
#    not merely the 3:1 muted-text floor this role technically only needs.
color.section_header=0x0066BB66
color.text_muted=0x00A0A6B0

# --- #711 Loop 3 / Family 6 (Terminal): banner/prompt palette -------------
# Director spec (section 2 item 4): map the terminal's chrome-level banner
# and prompt text through the theme instead of the raw ANSI escape codes
# userland/apps/terminal/main.c currently hardcodes for them. The general
# 16-slot ANSI table (ansi_colors[]/st_pal[]) is UNCHANGED and stays
# semantic, per the existing in-source comment at main.c:180 ("ANSI colors
# stay semantic; only the *default* fg/bg route through theme") - these
# three new keys are only for the two UI-authored escape sequences that
# were mistakenly drawing FROM that semantic table instead of from a
# theme token: the welcome banner ("\033[1;36m...") and the prompt's two
# segments ("\033[32muser@maytera...:\033[34m<cwd>...").
#
# Dark: KEPT AT CURRENT VALUES per director instruction, verified not
# assumed. Measured (WCAG relative-luminance contrast, same metric every
# other family in this file uses) against color.surface_sunken/textbox_bg
# (0x121216, the terminal's actual default background via
# THEME_COLOR_TEXTBOX_BG):
#   term_banner      (current raw \033[1;36m = bright cyan 0x55FFFF) 15.25:1 PASS
#   term_prompt_user (current raw \033[32m   = green      0x00AA00)  6.01:1 PASS
# Both clear 4.5:1 with wide margin - director's "dark keeps current
# values" is confirmed correct for these two, not just assumed.
#
# term_prompt_path is a THIRD segment the director's two example values
# did not cover (the cwd text after the colon, raw \033[34m = dark blue
# 0x0000AA). Measured it anyway per this family's evidence-bar duty
# ("verify every default output color... sampled not eyeballed") and
# found a real, severe defect the director's spec did not know about:
# 1.41:1 against 0x121216, i.e. functionally invisible dark-blue-on-
# near-black. Not a light-scheme problem (13.29:1 on white there, fine) -
# a DARK-scheme-only regression sitting in the same two prompt lines the
# director was already fixing. Fixed here by pointing it at the existing
# color.link_color token (0x5AA9FF, already AA-checked for body text
# elsewhere in this file) rather than inventing a new hex: 7.61:1 on
# 0x121216. Not asked for; flagged as a correction, see report.
color.term_banner=0x0055FFFF        # = current raw ANSI bright-cyan value, unchanged
color.term_prompt_user=0x0000AA00   # = current raw ANSI green value, unchanged
color.term_prompt_path=0x005AA9FF   # = color.link_color (NEW: fixes 1.41:1 -> 7.61:1)
#
# STATUS: data only, AWAITING ENGINEERING WIRING. No reader exists yet
# (checked, 0 grep hits): kernel/gui/themes.h's TM_* enum / kernel/gui/
# themes.c's g_theme_fields table have no entries for these three keys,
# and userland/libc/theme.h's THEME_COLOR_* enum has no TERM_BANNER /
# TERM_PROMPT_USER / TERM_PROMPT_PATH slots for SYS_THEME_COLOR to serve.
# Minimal wiring spec for Substrate: add the three enum members + offset-
# table rows (same mechanism the loop-2 titlebar-button tokens used), then
# in userland/apps/terminal/main.c replace the two hardcoded escape
# writes:
#   term_puts("\033[1;36m") -> a direct cell-attribute write using
#     theme_color(THEME_COLOR_TERM_BANNER) instead of routing through
#     ansi_colors[14], for the banner line only (main.c ~1353-1355).
#   print_prompt()'s two term_puts("\033[32m"/"\033[34m") calls -> the
#     same direct-color substitution for THEME_COLOR_TERM_PROMPT_USER /
#     THEME_COLOR_TERM_PROMPT_PATH (main.c ~536-538). The trailing
#     "\033[0m" resets stay as-is (SGR reset has no colour to theme).
# kernel/gui/terminal.c and kernel/gui/sshterm.c (compositor-absent
# kernel-side terminal fallbacks, fully hardcoded, no .mtheme reader at
# all) are NOT covered by this - see report for why they are out of
# scope this loop.

# --- #711 Loop 3 / Family 1 (window decorations): elevation shadow + outer
# corner radius (director redirect, section 1 + Substrate priority 1) ------
# NEW this loop. Reverses the earlier "window outer corners are always 0,
# frames are opaque kernel rects, no alpha compositing to round against"
# ruling from loop 1/substrate (see this file's own "radius.* :" comment
# above and the surfaces family's "there are no shadows in this system,
# removed by #189" note further down): the director's loop-3 redirect
# explicitly asks for both, scored as the single largest item in both loop-2
# reviews, so I checked whether it is actually renderable before writing a
# spec that hopes it is.
#
# IT IS RENDERABLE WITH ZERO NEW PRIMITIVES. kernel/gui/window_decor.c
# already ships decor_fill_rounded_rect(x,y,w,h,radius,color) - a real
# per-pixel filled rounded rect (corner test is a distance-squared circle
# fill, not a stroked Bresenham outline, so it does not hit the "circles
# below 14px look ragged" limit that applies to OUTLINES) - and it is
# already used in production by kernel/gui/login.c for the login input box
# and the avatar circle, INCLUDING an offset solid-colour shadow copy drawn
# first (decor_fill_rounded_rect(x+2,y+3,w,h,h/2,LOGIN_SHADOW_COLOR) then
# the real surface on top, login.c:211-212). kernel/gui/window.c's own
# wm_draw_winmenu() does the same offset-silhouette trick for the popup menu
# today (fb_fill_rect(x+3,y+3,w,h,0x00101418) then the popup body,
# window.c:1265-1266). window.c's TOP-LEVEL frame draw currently uses a
# plain fb_fill_rect with no radius parameter at all (window.c:~1358+) -
# that call site is the only thing that needs to change; the corner-fill
# and offset-shadow techniques it needs already exist and are proven.
#
# radius.window: outer window corner radius, decor_fill_rounded_rect's
# radius argument. This theme is decor.style=gradient, so corners round.
radius.window=4
#
# decor.window_shadow / shadow.* : an OPAQUE, hard-edged offset silhouette,
# same mechanism as login.c's LOGIN_SHADOW_COLOR box and wm_draw_winmenu's
# edge band - never a blur/translucency effect on live content (renderer
# limit, unchanged). "blur" here is NOT a convolution (this renderer cannot
# draw one): it means a 3-STEP RAMP of nested decor_fill_rounded_rect calls
# at radius.window, each offset ~shadow.offset_y/3 px further than the last,
# each drawn via the existing fb_fill_rect_alpha (framebuffer.c:559,
# already proven at FULL-SCREEN scale by login.c's own LOGIN_SCRIM_ALPHA
# scrim - a per-window small region is comfortably inside the "small
# regions only" alpha exception, nowhere near the banned full-screen-scrim
# case) with alpha stepping down from shadow.alpha toward 0 across
# shadow.blur px of total spread. This is the director's own "3-step
# darkened ramp against the sampled backdrop is acceptable for loop 3;
# zero shadow is not" allowance, expressed as concrete numbers Substrate
# can implement directly, reusing decor_fill_rounded_rect + fb_fill_rect_
# alpha exactly as they already exist - no new kernel primitive to build.
# Modal windows (window_t already carries a MODAL flag) use shadow.modal_
# alpha/shadow.modal_blur instead of the base pair; offset_y is shared.
decor.window_shadow=1
shadow.color=0x00000000
shadow.alpha=90
shadow.offset_y=4
shadow.blur=12
shadow.modal_alpha=120
shadow.modal_blur=16
#
# Superseded by the above, not edited in place (not this addition's paragraph
# to rewrite, and a second definition of the same key would be ambiguous for
# this line-reader parser): this file's own "radius.* :" comment near the
# top ("Window OUTER corners are always 0 ... no alpha compositing to round
# it against") and the surfaces family's "there are no shadows in this
# system... removed by #189" note further down. Both were correct as of
# loop 1; the director's loop-3 redirect is the reason they no longer are.
# Flagged here rather than silently contradicted.

# --- #711 Loop 3 (buttons family): audit only, no data changes ------------
# Full independent re-verification pass over every btn.*/state.btn_* pair
# already authored in Loop 1/2 above, plus a precise re-diagnosis of the
# engineering gap those loops already flagged. No color.*/state.*/btn.* key
# below this comment is added, removed or changed in value this loop.
#
# Contrast re-verified by hand (WCAG relative luminance), not re-typed from
# a prior loop's report:
#   btn.danger.rest/hover/active vs on_danger .......... 4.58:1 (matches L1/L2)
#   btn.primary.hover.bg vs on_accent (tightest pair) ... 4.73:1 (matches L1/L2)
#   btn.segmented.rest.fg vs .rest.bg .................... 6.03:1 (close to L1's
#     5.94:1, rounding only - see honesty note below)
# All pass AA (4.5:1 body text floor). Honesty note: the first hand pass at
# the segmented pair this loop produced a false "2.27:1 FAIL" from a single
# decimal slip (0.7152*0.01765 mistyped as 0.126232, a factor-of-10 error).
# Caught by redoing the arithmetic before acting on it, not after; no data
# was changed on the strength of the wrong number. Recorded per this repo's
# "verify the artifact" standard rather than silently discarded.
#
# Cross-family finding, attribution corrected, not fixed here: the forms
# family's Loop 3 note (docs/UI_STYLE_GUIDE.md) flags color.accent_active at
# 2.42:1 against color.surface_sunken as "shared with the button family's
# pressed state." Recomputed independently: 2.42:1 confirmed exactly (dark
# scheme only - the same pair in maytera_light.mtheme measures 7.40:1,
# fine). But checked every accent_active use in THIS file's btn.* block
# (rest/hover.border, active.bg, toolbar/pill/segmented selected.border):
# none of them place accent_active against surface_sunken - button fills
# only ever sit against surface/surface_raised, or flush against their own
# border (bg==border by construction). The pairing that actually measures
# 2.42:1 is state.radio_ring_active (=accent_active) against state.radio_bg
# (=surface_sunken), a forms-family control, not a button. Redirecting the
# attribution so this is not chased twice against the wrong control; not
# mine to fix either way (shared hex + another family's geometry section).
#
# Engineering gap, reconfirmed not re-argued: kernel/gui/themes.c's
# g_theme_fields[] still has 0 entries for any btn.<variant>.* key or the
# five state.btn_*_border/selected_focus_ring keys (re-grepped this loop,
# unchanged since Loop 2). Sharper than Loop 2's own "C2" spec: that ask
# ("make gui_draw_button() read theme->s_btn_*") is not achievable as
# written - theme_t is a KERNEL struct, and the actual live button
# primitive, userland/libc/gui.c's gui_button(), is Ring 3 code that can
# only reach kernel colour data through the closed 66-entry THEME_COLOR_*
# enum (kernel/proc/syscall.c's theme_get_color_by_id() switch, read
# line-by-line: ids 0-65 only, no state.btn_*/btn.<variant>.* id exists at
# any layer userland can reach). gui_button() draws from gui_palette_t (10
# fields: surface/surface_raised/ink/ink_dim/accent/accent_hover/border/
# field_bg/field_border/track - no per-state slot, no per-state border, no
# focus_ring field at all), populated per-app by 25 separate
# gui_set_palette() call sites, most from a private hardcoded per-theme
# table in that app's own source (same class of gap Family 3 found for
# form controls, reconfirmed here for buttons). gui_style_sync_from_theme()
# only syncs g_style (radius/gradient/decor) - it never touches g_pal.
# Corrected wiring spec, three necessary parts, not one:
#   1. New SYS_THEME_COLOR ids (or a new syscall) exposing state.btn_*/
#      btn.<variant>.* through theme_get_color_by_id()'s switch - same
#      aliasing technique already used for color.accent (id 2).
#   2. Extend gui_palette_t (userland/libc/gui_style.h) with the per-state/
#      border/focus_ring slots gui_button() needs - it cannot structurally
#      express a distinct pressed border or focus ring today, regardless
#      of what any .mtheme file says.
#   3. Give gui_style_sync_from_theme() (or a new call) the job of actually
#      filling gui_palette_t from the new ids, and audit the 25 existing
#      gui_set_palette() call sites off their private per-app tables.
# Not buildable from a data-only pass; flagged precisely so a future
# engineering loop is one pass, not a rediscovery.
#
# LIVE, CONFIRMED defect (read directly off the shipping source, not
# deferred or theoretical): gui_button()'s focus ring draws in p->accent
# unconditionally - userland/libc/gui.c, modern style ~line 649
# (gui_rounded_border(handle,x,y,w,h,GUI_BTN_RADIUS,p->accent)) and classic
# style ~line 650 (gui_draw_rect_outline(handle,x,y,w,h,p->accent)). For
# GUI_BTN_PRIMARY the button's own FILL is also p->accent (~line 611). A
# focused primary/default button draws its ring in the exact same colour as
# its own fill - an invisible ring, on every app using gui_button() with
# GUI_BTN_PRIMARY, in both maytera themes, today. This is exactly the B9
# defect Loop 2 already fixed IN THE DATA (state.btn_selected_focus_ring=
# color.on_accent, above) - that fix has had no way to reach the screen
# because gui_button() reads neither state.btn_selected_focus_ring nor any
# per-variant token, only the 4-colour gui_palette_t. Top-priority item once
# the wiring above lands: add a focus_ring field to gui_palette_t, and for
# GUI_BTN_PRIMARY/GUI_BTN_SUCCESS use an on-fill-colour ring instead of
# p->accent, matching what state.btn_selected_focus_ring already specifies.

# --- #745: taskbar-surface ink -----------------------------------------------
# The Settings left nav paints its labels, icons and version line on
# taskbar_bg / taskbar_hover / taskbar_active. Before these three keys existed
# it borrowed label_text (contracted against window_bg), button_disabled
# (against button_bg) and menu_text_disabled (against menu_bg), which on a
# theme with a light window and a dark bar rendered dark ink on a dark
# surface. These state this theme's own ink for that surface.
# Checked at build time by build/assets/theme-scale-lint.sh check #9 and at
# load time by kernel/gui/themes.c theme_ensure_all_contrast().
taskbar_text=0x00F2F3F5
taskbar_text_muted=0x00AEB4BE
taskbar_selected_text=0x00FFFFFF

# --- #745 glass chrome ---------------------------------------------------
# The MATERIAL of the taskbar / panel / dock / start menu. Per-theme, because
# the tint is a colour decision; the OPACITY is not here and never will be,
# because opacity is a USER preference (dock_opacity in UIPROFIL.YML, driven by
# Settings -> Appearance -> Transparency -> Dock). There is deliberately no
# glass_alpha key, so switching theme can never overwrite a number the user set.
#
# glass_enable defaults to 0 for style=retro themes (retro_unix, classic,
# high_contrast): translucency is wrong on principle for the beveled CDE/Win95
# lineage. An explicit key here always wins over that default.
#
# (#745 dockgrey, 2026-08-12) USER-REPORTED: the marble dock/taskbar read as
# black, not dark grey, and were too opaque. glass_tint was 0x0014161A
# (20,22,26) - near-black, and noticeably darker than this theme's own
# CLR_MENU_BG (0x2C2C35, 44,44,53, the weather-widget reference the user
# named). Recomputed with the same "settle toward black" formula
# glass_theme_apply() (main.c) now uses for every theme that does not set this
# key explicitly (58% -> 78% darken of taskbar_bg=0x24242B), so this theme's
# explicit value tracks the shared derivation instead of drifting from it.
# See /tmp/dockgrey_harness.py for the 14-theme contrast table this was
# chosen from; g_dock_opacity's default also moved 90 -> 75 (draw.c) in the
# same change.
glass_tint=0x001C1C21
glass_enable=1

# The two launcher chips (Start, Maytera). ACHROMATIC BY REQUIREMENT and
# identical in both themes: they are OPAQUE, which is what makes their icon
# contrast (11.58:1 open, 14.33:1 rest, 17.77:1 hover) a property of the design
# instead of a property of the user's wallpaper.
#
# They are deliberately NOT derived from start_button. That key IS the accent
# in every shipping modern theme (0x2563C9 here), and decoupling from it is the
# whole point; start_button keeps its meaning for the other dock styles.
#
# chrome_chip_border is not decorative. A light chip on a LIGHT panel has no
# edge at all (1.15:1 analytic against light glass); this 1px border recovers it
# at 4.01:1, and on dark glass it is harmless because the fill already separates
# the chip at 6.52:1. One border colour, one rule, both themes.
chrome_chip_rest=0x00E4E7EB
chrome_chip_hover=0x00FFFFFF
chrome_chip_open=0x00CCD1D8
chrome_chip_border=0x004A5058
chrome_chip_bevel=0x009AA0A8
chrome_chip_ink=0x0016181C
